from catalog import L, save
L(3,'01','Hundreds, tens and ones','Numbers through 999',[24],
'Read, count, write and explain numbers through 999.',
'The friends label a pretend library inventory. Why is the zero in 406 important?',
'The places in a three-digit number are hundreds, tens and ones. 10 ones = 1 ten; 10 tens = 1 hundred. Count to 999 with these places.',
'406 = 400 + 0 + 6: four hundreds, no tens, six ones. 999 = 900 + 90 + 9.',
'Make a hundreds-tens-ones mat. Use paper bundles to show numbers named by a partner. Include numbers with zero.',
'hundreds; place; digit','Inventory data are fictional. Zero holds an empty place.',
'Write six hundred and thirty-two in digits.=>632||Expand 508.=>500 + 0 + 8||What is the value of 7 in 374?=>70||Complete: 897, 898, __, __.=>899, 900||Write 999 in words.=>nine hundred and ninety-nine||Explain 305 compared with 350.=>305 has zero tens and five ones; 350 has five tens and zero ones.')
L(3,'02','Compare and order','Numbers through 999',[24],
'Order three-digit numbers using < and >.',
'Amina compares team totals. Which place should she compare first?',
'Compare hundreds, then tens if needed, then ones. The sign < means less than; > means greater than. The open end faces the greater value.',
'427 > 398 because 4 hundreds exceed 3 hundreds. 452 < 459 because hundreds and tens match but 2 ones are fewer than 9.',
'Build three numbers from digit cards. Order them with comparison cards. Tell a story where number ordering helps.',
'less than; greater than; order','Read comparisons aloud in both directions. Use place-value reasoning.',
'Write < or >: 306 __ 360.=> <||Write < or >: 812 __ 799.=> >||Order: 507, 570, 475.=>475, 507, 570||Write a number between 640 and 650.=>Any whole number 641 to 649.||Greatest of 908, 890, 980?=>980||Give one use of ordering numbers.=>For example finding the largest stock total.')
L(3,'03','Name equal parts','Fractions',[25],
'Read fractions of shapes and collections.',
'Zainab divides a strip equally. What does each number in a fraction tell us?',
'In 1/3, denominator 3 counts equal parts in the whole; numerator 1 counts selected parts. Use halves, thirds, quarters, fifths and sixths.',
'One fifth of 10 tops: share into 5 equal groups, giving 2 each. One fifth is 2. Four sixths means select 4 of 6 equal parts.',
'Fold marked strips into 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 equal sections. Label a selected part. Use sets that divide equally.',
'numerator; denominator; third; fifth; sixth','Use equal-sized wholes. Parts must have equal amounts.',
'In 2/5, how many parts make the whole?=>5||In 3/4, how many parts are selected?=>3||Find 1/3 of 12.=>4||Find 1/6 of 12.=>2||Draw 5 equal parts and shade 2. Name the fraction.=>2/5||Why are unequal pieces unsuitable for thirds?=>Thirds require three equal parts.','fractionstrip')
L(3,'04','Equivalent and ordered fractions','Fractions',[25],
'Recognise equivalent fractions and compare fractions.',
'Musa folds equal-sized paper into halves and quarters. Can different fraction names show the same amount?',
'Equivalent fractions represent equal amounts of the same whole. One half equals two quarters. With equal denominators, more parts means more amount. For unit fractions, more equal parts in a whole means smaller pieces.',
'1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6. Also 1/3 > 1/6 and 3/5 > 2/5. Compare equal-sized wholes.',
'Overlay equal-length folded strips. Two quarters cover a half. Compare thirds with sixths using the same whole.',
'equivalent; compare; same whole','Always identify the whole. Half a large sheet may exceed half a small one.',
'Complete: 1/2 = __/4.=>2||Complete: 1/2 = __/6.=>3||Write < or >: 1/4 __ 1/2.=> <||Write < or >: 4/5 __ 2/5.=> >||Order 1/2, 1/6, 1/3 from least.=>1/6, 1/3, 1/2||Why is 1/3 greater than 1/6 of the same whole?=>Three equal parts are larger individually than six equal parts.','equivalent')
L(3,'05','Add three-digit numbers','Addition',[26],
'Add with regrouping and add three amounts in stages.',
'Daniel counts reusable paper sheets. How can an estimate help check his total?',
'Align place values. Add ones, exchange ten ones for a ten if needed. Add tens, exchange ten tens for a hundred if needed. Add hundreds. Add three amounts in stages.',
'268 + 157: 8 + 7 = 15 ones, write 5 and exchange 1 ten. Tens: 6 + 5 + 1 = 12, write 2 and exchange 1 hundred. Hundreds: 2 + 1 + 1 = 4. Total 425.',
'Build a sum on a place-value mat. Record exchanges. A partner checks by subtracting an addend from the total.',
'regroup; estimate; addend','Warm up with two-digit regrouping. Explain every exchange.',
'47 + 28 = __.=>75||236 + 142 = __.=>378||368 + 257 = __.=>625||125 + 75 + 40 = __.=>240||Classes collect 178 and 146 sheets. Total?=>324 sheets||Explain a check for 268 + 157 = 425.=>425 − 157 = 268, or recalculate with reversed addends.')
L(3,'06','Add fractions','Addition',[26],
'Add fractions with the same denominator.',
'Amina shades equal parts in two colours. How can she name the total?',
'When denominators match, parts have the same size. Add numerators and keep the denominator. Do not add denominators. Keep the same whole.',
'1/5 + 2/5 = 3/5. One fifth and two fifths make three fifths. 1/4 + 3/4 = 4/4 = 1 whole.',
'Divide a strip into six equal parts. Shade 1 blue and 3 green, without overlap. Total 4/6; show it is also 2/3.',
'like denominators; fraction; whole','Model selected parts without overlap for addition inside one whole.',
'1/4 + 2/4 = __.=>3/4||2/6 + 3/6 = __.=>5/6||1/3 + 1/3 = __.=>2/3||2/5 + 3/5 = __.=>1 whole, or 5/5||Shade 1/6 then 2/6. Total?=>3/6, or 1/2||Why keep the denominator?=>Part size stays the same; the count of parts changes.')
L(3,'07','Subtract three-digit numbers','Subtraction',[27],
'Subtract with exchanges and in stages.',
'Musa uses sheets from a stock. What stays the same when he exchanges a hundred for ten tens?',
'Keep the total value unchanged in exchanges. Subtract ones, tens, then hundreds. If a story removes two amounts, remove the first and then the second.',
'432 − 178: exchange a ten to get 12 ones and 2 tens. 12 − 8 = 4. Exchange a hundred to get 12 tens and 3 hundreds. 12 − 7 = 5; 3 − 1 = 2. Answer 254.',
'Build 325. Remove 146 with place-value pieces. Check remaining 179 by adding 179 + 146.',
'exchange; difference; stages','With zero tens, show both exchanges explicitly.',
'63 − 28 = __.=>35||578 − 243 = __.=>335||502 − 178 = __.=>324||400 − 125 − 75 = __.=>200||346 sheets: use 128. How many left?=>218 sheets||Check 432 − 178 = 254.=>254 + 178 = 432.')
L(3,'08','Subtract fractions','Subtraction',[27],
'Subtract fractions with the same denominator.',
'Zainab shades five sixths then erases two sixths. How much stays shaded?',
'With like denominators, subtract numerators and keep the denominator. Equal-part size stays the same. Check using addition.',
'5/6 − 2/6 = 3/6 = 1/2. Check: 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6.',
'Fold a strip into equal parts. Shade some and cross out a few shaded parts. Write and check the subtraction.',
'numerator; denominator; remain','The whole strip keeps the same number of equal parts after selected parts are removed.',
'3/4 − 1/4 = __.=>2/4, or 1/2||4/5 − 2/5 = __.=>2/5||5/6 − 1/6 = __.=>4/6, or 2/3||3/3 − 2/3 = __.=>1/3||4/6 shaded: erase 3/6. Remaining?=>1/6||How does addition check 4/5 − 1/5 = 3/5?=>3/5 + 1/5 = 4/5.')
L(3,'09','Multiplication facts to nine','Multiplication',[28],
'Build and recall facts through 9 × 9.',
'Daniel makes a dot array. What happens when he turns it?',
'Turning a 3-by-4 array makes 4-by-3; both contain 12. Use known facts to build others. Practise every fact in the 1-to-9 table on the reference page.',
'6 × 7 = (5 × 7) + 7 = 35 + 7 = 42. 9 × 8 = (10 × 8) − 8 = 72.',
'Choose a fact from the reference table. Build it with counters, cover the answer, say it and check. Swap factors to see the same total.',
'factor; product; array','Use patterns and objects before recall. Speed alone is not understanding.',
'7 × 6 = __.=>42||8 × 8 = __.=>64||9 × 9 = __.=>81||4 × 7 = __.=>28||Use 5 × 8 to find 6 × 8.=>40 + 8 = 48||Why does 3 × 9 = 9 × 3?=>Turning the array swaps rows and columns but preserves 27 dots.','array34')
L(3,'10','Multiply larger amounts','Multiplication',[28],
'Multiply two digits by one digit and three one-digit factors.',
'Amina puts 24 paper squares in each of three envelopes. How could splitting 24 help?',
'Split a two-digit number into tens and ones. Multiply each part and add products. With three one-digit factors, multiply two first, then the third.',
'24 × 3 = (20 × 3) + (4 × 3) = 60 + 12 = 72. Also 2 × 3 × 4 = 6 × 4 = 24.',
'Build three groups of 12 with bundles and loose strips. Record tens and ones products, then combine. Try four groups of three pairs.',
'partial product; factor; stages','Explain grouping with objects before a compact written algorithm.',
'12 × 4 = __.=>48||23 × 3 = __.=>69||36 × 2 = __.=>72||2 × 4 × 3 = __.=>24||Four teams need 15 cards each. Total?=>60 cards||Explain 27 × 3 with tens and ones.=>20 × 3 = 60 and 7 × 3 = 21; total 81.')
L(3,'11','Share and group to divide','Division',[29],
'Divide exact totals up to 48 by 2 to 6.',
'Musa shares 24 tops among four teams. Is he finding group size or number of groups?',
'Division shares equally or counts equal groups. The sign ÷ means divided by. Multiplication undoes exact division. Here no objects are left over.',
'24 ÷ 4 = 6. Sharing among 4 teams gives 6 each. Grouping 24 into fours gives 6 groups. Check 4 × 6 = 24.',
'Use 12, 24 or 36 counters. Choose 2, 3, 4 or 6 groups that divide exactly. Share, record and check.',
'divide; share; groups; quotient','Ask which quantity the answer describes. Assessed totals are at most 48 with divisors 2–6.',
'18 ÷ 3 = __.=>6||48 ÷ 6 = __.=>8||40 ÷ 5 = __.=>8||28 ÷ 4 = __.=>7||Share 32 tops among 4 teams. How many each?=>8 tops||Check 30 ÷ 5 = 6 by multiplication.=>5 × 6 = 30.')
L(3,'12','Factors and multiples','Division',[29],
'Find factors, missing factors and multiples.',
'Zainab arranges 12 counters in rows. Which row sizes leave none over?',
'A factor divides a whole number exactly. Factors of 12 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. A multiple is a number multiplied by a whole number. First positive multiples of 4: 4, 8, 12, 16.',
'3 × __ = 18 has missing factor 6. Correct factor pairs of 30 include 1 × 30, 2 × 15, 3 × 10 and 5 × 6.',
'Make all rectangular arrays for 12 counters. Record factor pairs. Count the first five positive multiples of 3.',
'factor; multiple; factor pair','The legacy text has an incorrect factor example for 30. Use checked products here.',
'List all factors of 8.=>1, 2, 4, 8||First four positive multiples of 5?=>5, 10, 15, 20||4 × __ = 28.=>7||Is 3 a factor of 20?=>No||Is 24 a multiple of 6? Explain.=>Yes; 6 × 4 = 24.||Factor or multiple of 6: what is the difference?=>A factor divides 6 exactly; a multiple is 6 times a whole number.')
L(3,'13','Balance the number sentence','Open sentences',[30],
'Find unknowns using relationships between operations.',
'Daniel makes both sides of a sentence equal. Can the blank come before equals?',
'Both sides must have the same value. Addition undoes subtraction; multiplication undoes exact division. Use an inverse operation and substitute to check.',
'__ − 125 = 240: add 125 to 240 to get 365. Check 365 − 125 = 240. In 6 × __ = 42, use 42 ÷ 6 = 7.',
'Write true sentences, replace a number with a blank, and swap puzzles. Explain a related addition/subtraction pair with an everyday story.',
'unknown; inverse; substitute','Encourage several strategies. A repeated unknown in one equation keeps the same value.',
'__ + 138 = 400.=>262||520 − __ = 275.=>245||__ − 125 = 240.=>365||6 × __ = 42.=>7||18 ÷ __ = 3.=>6||Explain 50 = 32 + __.=>18, since 50 − 32 = 18 and 32 + 18 = 50.')
L(3,'14','Money calculations','Money',[31],
'Exchange, add, subtract and multiply money values to ₦20.',
'Amina checks pretend receipts. What should a correct receipt show?',
'These historical curriculum amounts are pretend exercises. Add costs, subtract for change and multiply equal costs. Read each card’s value. These are not actual market prices.',
'Four pretend items at ₦3 each cost 4 × ₦3 = ₦12. Pay ₦20: change ₦8. Check ₦12 + ₦8 = ₦20.',
'Use paper value cards in shop role-play. Write count, unit cost, total, payment and change. Exchange equivalent values with partners.',
'unit cost; total cost; exchange','Keep products at or below ₦20. Genuine denomination examples need supervising adult verification.',
'3 items at ₦4 each cost __.=>₦12||Pay ₦20 for ₦12 cost. Change?=>₦8||Exchange ₦20 for ₦5 cards. How many?=>4||Pretend costs ₦8 and ₦7 total __.=>₦15||5 items at ₦4 each cost __.=>₦20||Check change of ₦5 after paying ₦20 for ₦15.=>₦15 + ₦5 = ₦20.')
L(3,'15','Measure lengths accurately','Length',[32,33],
'Measure dimensions in cm/m and explain standard units.',
'Musa measures a garden label. Where must the ruler’s zero go?',
'Length, width and height are dimensions. Place zero at one end and read the other end. Use a tape or metre stick for longer distances. Body units vary; standard units allow fair comparisons.',
'A strip from 0 cm to 8 cm is 8 cm long. A strip from 2 cm to 9 cm is 9 − 2 = 7 cm long.',
'Measure a book’s length and width with a real ruler. Repeat in hand spans. Explain why standard results should agree more closely. Find another use for a measured length.',
'dimension; width; height; standard','The schematic ruler is not actual size. Use a physical ruler and its zero mark.',
'Strip from 0 cm to 12 cm: length?=>12 cm||Strip from 3 cm to 11 cm: length?=>8 cm||2 m = __ cm.=>200 cm||Choose a convenient unit for door height.=>m; cm also valid if accurately used.||Measure your book’s length and width.=>Two plausible readings with cm units.||Why do standard units help comparisons?=>Their size is agreed and does not vary by person.','ruler')
L(3,'16','Distance around the edge','Length',[32],
'Measure and calculate perimeters.',
'Zainab puts string around a paper garden plan. What length is needed?',
'Perimeter is the distance all around a shape. Add every outside side once using the same unit. Opposite sides of a rectangle are equal.',
'A rectangle 6 cm long and 4 cm wide has perimeter 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 20 cm. A square of side 3 m has perimeter 12 m.',
'Trace a book cover. Measure and add all its outside sides. Check with string, then straighten and measure it. Discuss a frame or garden boundary.',
'perimeter; boundary; side','Perimeter is length, not area. Use cm or m rather than square units.',
'Square side 5 cm: perimeter?=>20 cm||Rectangle 8 cm by 3 cm: perimeter?=>22 cm||Triangle sides 4, 5, 6 cm: perimeter?=>15 cm||Rectangle 9 m by 2 m: perimeter?=>22 m||Give one everyday use of perimeter.=>For example measuring edging, a boundary or a frame.||Why is 6 + 4 not the 6 cm by 4 cm rectangle’s perimeter?=>It counts only two sides; all four total 20 cm.','perimeter')
L(3,'17','Hours and minutes','Time',[33],
'Read minutes and calculate simple intervals.',
'Daniel times an activity. How many minutes make an hour?',
'1 hour = 60 minutes. On an analogue clock each number step is 5 minutes. The minute hand at 3 means 15 past; at 6, 30; at 9, 45. The hour hand moves gradually.',
'At 2:15 the minute hand is at 3; the hour hand is just past 2. From 2:10 to 2:35 is 25 minutes.',
'Show 1:05, 1:20 and 1:45 on a paper clock. Name a use of time measurement in cooking, travel or school.',
'minute; hour; interval','Start with same-hour intervals, then cross an hour on a number line.',
'1 hour = __ minutes.=>60||Write quarter past five in digits.=>5:15||At 4:45 where is the minute hand?=>9||From 8:10 to 8:35: how long?=>25 minutes||9:50 plus 20 minutes: finish time?=>10:10||Why is the hour hand between 3 and 4 at 3:30?=>Half the hour has passed; it has moved halfway to 4.')
L(3,'18','Read a calendar','Time',[33],
'Read calendar dates and explain their usefulness.',
'Amina marks a class event on a practice calendar. How does a date differ from a weekday?',
'A full date gives a day number, month and year. Weekdays repeat every seven days. Read the calendar heading first. Months have different lengths.',
'Our fictional practice month has 30 days and starts on Monday. Day 1 is Monday, as are days 8 and 15.',
'Make the practice month with Monday-to-Sunday columns. Fill days 1 to 30. Circle event dates. Explain why a real calendar helps plan appointments.',
'date; month; year; calendar','Use an actual calendar for real event dates. Our diagram is deliberately fictional.',
'Practice month: weekday on day 2?=>Tuesday||Weekday on day 8?=>Monday||One week after day 9: day number?=>16||How many days in two weeks?=>14||What three pieces make a full date?=>Day number, month, year||Why check the calendar month and year?=>The same date number can have different weekdays in different months and years.','calendar')
L(3,'19','Grams and kilograms','Weight',[34],
'Measure, compare and use grams and kilograms.',
'Musa checks a scale beside a grain bag. Which unit must he look for?',
'Everyday weight measurements use grams (g) and kilograms (kg). 1 kg = 1,000 g. Check the scale starts at zero and read its unit. Scientifically these units measure mass.',
'2 kg = 2,000 g. A 700 g object is heavier than a 500 g object by 200 g.',
'With an adult, weigh safe small objects, including smooth stones. Record readings and units. Discuss why agreed units help buyers and sellers.',
'gram; kilogram; scale; mass','No unknown minerals, dusty samples or heavy loads. Do not infer mineral identity by appearance.',
'1 kg = __ g.=>1,000 g||Heavier: 900 g or 1 kg?=>1 kg||Difference: 800 g and 300 g?=>500 g||3 kg = __ g.=>3,000 g||Stones of 250 g and 400 g: total?=>650 g||Why agree a unit in a transaction?=>Buyer and seller understand the same measured amount.')
L(3,'20','Measure in litres','Capacity',[35],
'Measure liquids accurately in litres.',
'Zainab fills a measuring jug for the garden. Where should her eyes be?',
'Litre, written L, is a standard unit for capacity and liquid volume. Place a graduated jug on a level surface. Read the liquid at eye height. Fill carefully to the mark.',
'3 L and 5 L total 8 L; the second is 2 L more. Half a litre plus half a litre makes 1 L.',
'With adult help, measure into a bucket using a marked 1 L container. Record litres up to a safe mark. Prevent spills and use clean water.',
'litre; graduated; level; capacity','Use plastic equipment. Do not taste liquids. Marks, not picture size, determine volume.',
'2 L + 4 L = __.=>6 L||5 L: pour out 2 L. Remaining?=>3 L||How many half-litres make 1 L?=>2||Three 2 L containers: total capacity?=>6 L||Unit for bucket capacity: L or cm?=>L||Explain accurate jug reading.=>Set it level and read the marked liquid level at eye height.')
L(3,'21','Straight, curved and symmetrical','Symmetry',[36],
'Recognise lines and find lines of symmetry.',
'Daniel folds a paper shape. When do both halves fit exactly?',
'A straight line does not bend; a curve bends. A line of symmetry makes mirror halves that match on folding. Check the whole outline, not just corners.',
'A non-square rectangle has 2 symmetry lines. A square has 4: two through opposite side midpoints and two diagonals. Not every triangle is symmetrical.',
'Fold squares and non-square rectangles along possible mirror lines. Draw every line that works. Compare with an adult-cut scalene triangle.',
'symmetry; mirror; straight; curve','A non-square rectangle’s diagonal is not a symmetry line. A circle has infinitely many.',
'How many square symmetry lines?=>4||How many in a non-square rectangle?=>2||Draw one straight line and one curve.=>An unbent line and a bending line.||Is every triangle symmetrical?=>No||Draw a rectangle’s two symmetry lines.=>Through opposite side midpoints.||Why does a non-square rectangle’s diagonal fail the fold test?=>The folded halves do not coincide.','symmetry')
L(3,'22','Draw and describe shapes','Symmetry',[36],
'Draw and compare squares, rectangles, triangles and circles.',
'Amina designs shape cards. Which properties remain when she turns them?',
'Use a ruler for straight sides. A square has four equal sides and four right angles. A rectangle has opposite sides equal and four right angles. A triangle has three straight sides. A circle has no straight sides or corners.',
'For a 4 cm by 2 cm rectangle: draw a 4 cm base, make right angles at each end, mark 2 cm sides, join the top endpoints. Check opposite sides.',
'Trace a round lid for a circle. Draw a square, a non-square rectangle and different triangles. Find approximate examples nearby.',
'property; side; right angle; draw','Test angles with a folded paper corner. Three curved sides do not make a triangle.',
'Draw a square of side 3 cm.=>Four 3 cm sides and four right angles.||Draw a 5 cm by 2 cm rectangle.=>Opposite sides 5 cm and 2 cm, four right angles.||Draw a triangle; mark its corners.=>Three straight sides, three marked vertices.||Trace a circle; mark no corners.=>Closed circular curve without corners.||How many right angles in a square?=>4||Why does a turned rectangle keep its name?=>Its side relationships and four right angles remain.')
L(3,'23','Read picture graphs','Pictograms',[37],
'Interpret horizontal and vertical pictograms.',
'Musa graphs invented game choices. What must be read before counting pictures?',
'A pictogram uses pictures for counts. Read title, labels and key. Horizontal pictograms use rows across; vertical ones use columns upwards. Here each dot means one child.',
'Practice counts: ball 4, skipping 3, clapping 2. Total 9. Ball has one more than skipping.',
'Read the graph with a partner. Rebuild it vertically with counters and the same key. Explain why totals stay the same.',
'pictogram; key; row; column','Other graphs can use a symbol for several items. Always read the key.',
'How many chose ball?=>4||How many chose clapping?=>2||How many choices altogether?=>9||How many more chose ball than clapping?=>2||Redraw as labelled vertical columns.=>Heights 4, 3, 2; key one dot per child.||Why is a key needed?=>It gives the number represented by a symbol.','pictogram')
L(3,'24','Make a graph and find the mode','Pictograms',[37],
'Collect, graph and interpret data; identify the mode.',
'Zainab wants to choose a class game fairly. What question could she ask?',
'Collect one answer per participant. Group answers. Make a pictogram with a clear key. The mode is the category occurring most. Results describe only the people asked.',
'Invented choices: ball, clapping, ball, skipping, clapping, ball. Ball 3, clapping 2, skipping 1. Ball is the mode.',
'Use invented data or a voluntary non-sensitive survey. Make a horizontal or vertical graph with title, labels and key. Check totals.',
'mode; most frequent; survey','Ties can produce several modal categories. Do not generalise a class survey to a whole town.',
'What is the worked data’s mode?=>ball||How many responses?=>6||Graph ball 3, clapping 2, skipping 1.=>Correct labelled graph; key one symbol per response.||How many more ball than skipping choices?=>2||Can two categories with 4 votes each both be modes?=>Yes, if 4 is the highest count.||Give a use of a class pictogram.=>For example comparing choices to plan an activity.')
save(3)
